<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> for adults, a beginner group lesson is the most common way to start, and a private lesson is the fastest way to improve. A first lesson covers gear, stance, stopping, turning, and riding the lift. Most adults can ski green runs within one to three days of lessons. Skiing is very learnable at any age.</p></div>

<h2>Group vs private lessons</h2>
<h3>Group lessons</h3>
<p>You're placed with other adults at a similar level, usually for a half day or full day. Group lessons are a social, lower-cost way to learn, and beginners benefit from watching others work through the same steps.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> first-timers, budget-conscious skiers, and anyone who enjoys learning with others.</li>
  <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> less individual attention, and the pace is set by the group.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Private lessons</h3>
<p>One instructor for you or your small group. The lesson is built around your goals, whether that's getting off the bunny hill or tackling moguls.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Best for:</strong> nervous beginners, people with limited days, and intermediates stuck on a specific skill.</li>
  <li><strong>Trade-off:</strong> higher cost. Splitting a private with a friend or partner at the same level helps.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Multi-day programs and clinics</h3>
<p>Many ski schools offer multi-day beginner packages or adult clinics that keep you with the same instructor and group. Consistency speeds up learning, and these programs often bundle a lift ticket and rentals.</p>

<h2>What your first lesson covers</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Gear check.</strong> Boot fit, buckling, stepping into bindings, and carrying skis.</li>
  <li><strong>Stance.</strong> An athletic position with shins pressed into the boots.</li>
  <li><strong>Sliding and stopping.</strong> The wedge (pizza) on a gentle slope. See <%= link_to "how to stop on skis", guide_path("how-to-stop-on-skis") %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Turning.</strong> Wedge turns to control speed and direction.</li>
  <li><strong>Lifts.</strong> Riding a magic carpet, then a chairlift.</li>
  <li><strong>Falling and getting up.</strong> Safely, without wasting energy.</li>
</ol>
<p>Want a preview before you go? Read <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %>.</p>

<h2>How long does it take to learn to ski?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> most adults can stop, make basic wedge turns, and ride a beginner lift.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 2–3:</strong> linking turns on green runs and trying easy blues.</li>
  <li><strong>A season or more:</strong> comfortable parallel turns on most blue terrain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Progress depends on fitness, prior board or skate experience, conditions, and how many days in a row you ski. Back-to-back days help skills stick.</p>

<h2>Is skiing hard?</h2>
<p>The first few hours are the hardest: stiff boots, slippery snow, and new movements. Once you can stop reliably, confidence builds quickly. Skiing is often considered easier to start than snowboarding but takes longer to master. Lessons, soft groomed snow, and the right-length rental skis make the start much easier. Use the <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> to check that your rentals fit.</p>

<h2>How to choose a ski school</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Look for adult-specific lessons.</strong> Many schools separate adults from kids, which keeps the pace and terrain right.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the beginner terrain.</strong> A dedicated learning area with a magic carpet and a slow beginner lift makes the first day far less intimidating.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask about certification.</strong> In the US, instructors may be certified through the Professional Ski Instructors of America (PSIA); in Canada, through the Canadian Ski Instructors' Alliance (CSIA).</li>
  <li><strong>Book ahead.</strong> Weekend and holiday lessons fill up.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a good day.</strong> Soft, freshly groomed snow is easier than ice. Check <%= link_to "where it's snowing this week", where_is_it_snowing_path %> when planning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tips for adult beginners</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Tell your instructor about nerves, injuries, or goals. They'll adapt.</li>
  <li>Take breaks, eat, and drink water, especially at altitude.</li>
  <li>Stop when you're tired. Most falls happen late in the day.</li>
  <li>Book a second lesson. One lesson starts the habits; a second locks them in.</li>
</ul>
